deep.reconstruction:
Section 7° opens with a recapitulative list of the group(-scheme) homomorphisms involving M_{ρ,σ} that were introduced in section 6°. Here M_{ρ,σ} is the group attached to the two elliptic (orbifold) points ρ (order 3, stabiliser of type Z/6) and σ (order 2, the point i, stabiliser of type Z/4) of the modular situation. Its group of integer points is computed on the previous page as Z/6×Z/4×{±1}, or Z/6×{±1}×{±1}.

Diagram (134) has two halves.
- Inputs: four central extensions by μ2 map into M_{ρ,σ}, namely μ2→SG, μ2→S'T_ρ, μ2→SN'_σ, μ2→G_m, through morphisms j_G, j_ρ, j_σ, j_μ. The next page calls them J_G, J_ρ, J_σ, J_μ.
- Outputs: M_{ρ,σ} has four fundamental morphisms:
  - Θ=Θ_G : M→G, followed by G→PG and det_G : G→G_m;
  - Θ_ρ : M→T_ρ, followed by det : T_ρ→G_m;
  - Θ_σ : M→N'_σ, followed by det : N'_σ→G_m and a sign character ε_σ : N'_σ→μ2;
  - ε_3 : M→μ2.
- In modern terms: G is a rank-2 (GL2-type) reductive group scheme. T_ρ is the (norm-type) torus attached to the order-3 point, i.e. the Z[ζ3] torus. N'_σ is the normaliser of the torus T_σ attached to i. SG, S'T_ρ and SN'_σ are kernels of determinants (or of the square of the determinant). PG = G/G_m.

Grothendieck then says the whole diagram can be rebuilt from minimal data (135):
- G_m →(i_ρ) T_ρ →(δ_ρ) G_m, with δ_ρ i_ρ(λ)=λ²;
- G_m →(i_σ) N'_σ →(δ_σ) G_m, with δ_σ i_σ(λ)=λ²;
- ε_σ : N'_σ→μ2, trivial on i_σ(G_m);
- plus the 'fundamental' 3×3 diagram (136). Its rows are 1→G_m→G→PG→1 and 1→μ2→SG→PG→1. Its columns are 1→μ2→G_m→G_m→1 (squaring), 1→SG→G→G_m→1 (det_G), and PG=PG.

The column compatibility is det(λ·I)=λ², i.e. rank 2. A first attempt at drawing this diagram is crossed out.

The next page completes the reconstruction. The S-groups are defined as kernels, and M_{ρ,σ} is defined as the quotient of SG×S'T_ρ×SN'_σ×G_m by the diagonally embedded μ2 (a central product). The four morphisms out of M are then described by their restrictions to the four factors, subject to commutation conditions and triviality on the diagonal μ2. The purpose is bookkeeping for the arithmetic of the modular orbifold (ρ, σ, cusp) in the 'équation des lacets' setting, where Galois/GT acts via M_{ρ,σ}(Ẑ)/Σ.
